# Answer 19 · Gendered Impacts of Floods and Heat Waves in Pakistan (CSS 2026)
@py question 2026-5

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Set out the two perspectives, use them to analyse the 2022 floods and recent heat waves, weigh what each explains, and end with policy.
@ask Climate change has GENDERED IMPACTS
@ask Using FEMINIST ENVIRONMENTALISM and ECOFEMINISM, CRITICALLY ANALYSE how recent FLOODS and HEAT WAVES in PAKISTAN affected WOMEN differently
(1) Introduction: climate disasters fall hardest on those with least land, money, mobility and voice — often women
(2) Why climate is a gender issue: exposure, assets, mobility, care and voice
(3) Ecofeminism: domination of women and nature linked (d'Eaubonne, Shiva)
(4) Feminist environmentalism: women's link to nature is material (Agarwal)
(5) The 2022 floods: scale and damage
(6) Floods and women (1): maternal health and camp safety
(7) Floods and women (2): livelihoods, relief access and identity cards
(8) Floods and women (3): care burdens, girls' schooling and early marriage
(9) Heat waves and women: kitchens, fields, pregnancy and water
(10) Critical analysis: what each perspective explains and misses; intersectionality
(11) Frameworks and policy: ccGAP, NDMA gender cell, BISP, loss and damage
(12) Conclusion: use both lenses; put women at the centre of climate policy
:::

## Decoding the question
The question names **two perspectives** — **feminist environmentalism** and **ecofeminism** — and a **case**: recent **floods and heat waves** in Pakistan. **"Critically analyse"** asks you to **use** both lenses to explain why women were affected differently, with evidence, and to **judge** which lens explains more. End with policy.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Climate change** — long-term shifts in temperature and weather caused mainly by greenhouse-gas emissions — is making floods and heat waves more frequent and severe. Its impacts are **gendered**: women and men are exposed, affected and able to recover in different ways.

#### ii) The argument
Pakistan's **2022 floods** and recent **heat waves** hit women harder because of their **work, assets, mobility, care duties and lack of voice**. **Ecofeminism** explains the shared logic of exploiting women and nature; **feminist environmentalism** better explains **which** women suffered most and **why**. Both are needed.

### 2. Why climate is a gender issue
Women in rural Pakistan depend daily on **water, fuel, fodder and livestock**; they own **little land** or savings; their **mobility** is often restricted; they carry most **care** for children, the sick and the elderly; and they have **little say** in planning and relief. Each of these turns a climate shock into a greater loss for women. Disasters do not create these inequalities; they **expose and deepen** them.

### 3. Ecofeminism
**Ecofeminism** holds that the **domination of women** and the **domination of nature** spring from the same patriarchal, profit-driven logic. The term was coined by **Françoise d'Eaubonne** (==1974==). **Vandana Shiva** (*Staying Alive*, ==1988==) argued that Western-style "maldevelopment" destroys forests, rivers and women's subsistence together, and that women are both the first victims and natural protectors of nature. Its symbols include the **Chipko** movement in India (==1973==).

### 4. Feminist environmentalism
**Bina Agarwal** ("The Gender and Environment Debate", ==1992==) criticised ecofeminism for suggesting women are **naturally** closer to nature. She argued that women's relation to the environment comes from their **material reality** — the gender division of labour, property and power. Women collect water and fuel **because of their assigned work**, and suffer from environmental damage **because they lack land and resources**. This is **feminist environmentalism**.

::: fig compare | Two feminist lenses on climate change.
## Ecofeminism
- One logic dominates women and nature
- Critique of destructive development
- Women as victims and protectors of nature
- d'Eaubonne 1974; Shiva 1988
- Risk: essentialism
## Feminist environmentalism
- Women's link to nature is material
- Work, property and power explain impact
- Focus on land, assets and voice
- Agarwal 1992
- Explains differences among women
:::

### 5. The 2022 floods
The monsoon floods of ==2022== affected about ==33 million== people and killed more than 1,700; about a third of the country was under water at the peak. The Post-Disaster Needs Assessment put damage and losses at about **30 billion dollars**. **UNFPA** estimated about ==650,000== pregnant women were in flood-affected areas.

### 6. Floods and women (1): health and safety

#### i) Maternal health
Clinics were destroyed or cut off; many women gave birth in camps or on roadsides without skilled help, and anaemia and malnutrition rose among pregnant women.

#### ii) Camp safety and privacy
Lack of separate toilets, bathing spaces and lighting exposed women and girls to harassment; many avoided eating and drinking to avoid open latrines. Menstrual hygiene supplies were scarce.

### 7. Floods and women (2): livelihoods and relief

#### i) Lost assets
Women lost **livestock** — often their main asset — kitchen gardens and home-based work, with little land or savings to recover.

#### ii) Relief access
Aid was often registered to **male heads of household** or required a **national identity card (CNIC)**, which many rural women lacked; women-headed households struggled to reach relief.

### 8. Floods and women (3): care, school and marriage
Women nursed children and elders through outbreaks of malaria, diarrhoea and skin disease. Schools were destroyed or used as shelters, and girls were more likely to drop out. Relief agencies reported cases of families marrying daughters early to reduce costs or for "protection" — a known pattern after disasters.

### 9. Heat waves and women
Pakistan is among the world's most heat-exposed countries; **Jacobabad** in Sindh is regularly among the hottest cities on earth. Women **cooking** over open fires in poorly ventilated kitchens and **working in fields** — picking cotton, harvesting — face high heat stress; **pregnant women** face higher risks; **water collection** takes longer as sources dry; and dress and mobility norms can make it harder to reach cooler places. Home-based workers in hot, crowded homes lose income. Heat also increases the burden of caring for children and elders who fall ill, and power cuts during heat waves make kitchens and homes even hotter.

### 10. Critical analysis

#### i) What ecofeminism explains
It exposes the common logic of **extractive development** that damages both nature and women, and it gives moral force to women's leadership in environmental protection. Pakistan, producing **less than 1%** of global emissions, suffers from a global economy built on exploiting nature — an ecofeminist point about justice.

#### ii) Its limits
Its suggestion that women share a natural bond with nature is **essentialist**, and it can romanticise rural women's heavy burdens.

#### iii) What feminist environmentalism explains
It explains **why** particular women suffered most — those without land, savings, CNICs, mobility or voice — and points to concrete remedies.

#### iv) Intersectionality
Poor, rural, landless, minority and disabled women suffered far more than urban, educated women — so neither "women" nor "nature" is a single category. Women-headed households and widows, with no male relative to register for aid, were among the hardest hit.

::: fig cycle | Why floods and heat hit women harder.
@centre Women's climate vulnerability
Dependence on water, fuel and livestock
Little land, savings or insurance
Restricted mobility; fewer phones and warnings
Care for children, the sick and the old
Health risks in pregnancy and heat
Little voice in relief and planning
:::

### 11. Frameworks and policy

#### i) Frameworks
Beijing's critical area **K** (women and the environment); the UNFCCC **Lima Work Programme on Gender** (==2014==) and **Gender Action Plan** (==2017==); Pakistan's **National Climate Change Policy** (updated ==2021==) and **Climate Change Gender Action Plan** (==2022==); the **NDMA Gender and Child Cell**; and the **loss and damage** fund agreed at **COP27** (==2022==).

#### ii) Policy
Collect **sex-disaggregated data**; put **women in disaster management**; provide **women-friendly relief** (separate toilets, lighting, female staff, maternal care); pay **cash to women** through **BISP** and help with CNICs; protect **land and asset rights**; send **early warnings** through channels women use, including Lady Health Workers; build **heat-safe** work and water near homes; and protect **girls' schooling**.

### 12. Conclusion
The floods and heat waves affected Pakistani women differently because climate shocks fall on an **unequal** society. **Ecofeminism** shows the shared roots of exploiting women and nature; **feminist environmentalism** shows how **work, property and power** decide who suffers. Used together, and with an intersectional eye, they point to one conclusion: climate policy in Pakistan must put **women's needs, rights and leadership at its centre**.

## Why this answer scores
- Both named perspectives are **explained and used**, not just defined.
- The **2022 floods** and **heat waves** are analysed with data and specific gendered impacts.
- The **critical analysis** weighs each lens and adds intersectionality.
- Two figures and a policy section turn analysis into recommendations.
